## Relationship to Other Mental Models

Management overlaps conceptually with [[planning]] and [[solutions]], both of which serve as navigational frameworks for understanding patterns and relationships in complex situations. The distinction between Management and these existing models is underspecified in the source, raising questions about whether it adds distinct value or functions as a catch-all label for the broader mental models cluster.^[raw/articles/Management.md]

Management connects to [[management-mental-model]] as a broader mental model, and to [[scatterfocus]] and [[hyperfocus---chris-bailey]] as complementary attention modes. It also relates to [[getting-things-done---david-allen]], [[deep-work - Cal Newport]], and [[principles---ray-dalio]] as foundational productivity sources.

## Open Questions

- What specific patterns or relationships does Management help recognize that [[planning]], [[solutions]], or [[stillness]] do not already cover?
- Does Management function as a distinct mental model or as a general label for the existing mental models framework?
- How should the claimed 30 cross-source references be reconciled with the absence of specific linked sources?

